WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.680
This is a picture from the&nbsp;
1930’s of Katherine Dewey.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:04.960
An American bobsledder.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.674
Back then, bobsleds looked kind of&nbsp;
like a toboggan crossed with a go-kart.

00:00:09.674 --> 00:00:12.732
Much different than the sleds&nbsp;
we see today.

00:00:12.732 --> 00:00:17.084
Dewey often competed as a pilot, or&nbsp;
driver, in 4-person bobsledding events.

00:00:17.084 --> 00:00:21.149
And in 1940, she won first place&nbsp;
in a national championship.

00:00:21.149 --> 00:00:24.606
And after that, women were&nbsp;banned 
from driving four-men in sleds.

00:00:24.606 --> 00:00:27.000
Like banned, you know, because&nbsp;
whatever our uterus would fall out

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.240
we wouldn't be able to have&nbsp;
babies, who the heck knows.&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.509
Yes... who knows.&nbsp;

00:00:33.469 --> 00:00:36.080
Dewey was never allowed to&nbsp;
compete in the Olympics.&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.962
In fact, it wasn’t until 2002 
that female&nbsp;bobsledders

00:00:39.962 --> 00:00:42.480
were invited to&nbsp;participate 
in the Games.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.716
But only in one event.

00:00:44.716 --> 00:00:48.099
Since 1932, men have had two.

00:00:48.099 --> 00:00:51.440
But this year a new women-only&nbsp;
event was introduced.&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:53.235
The monobob.

00:00:54.555 --> 00:00:57.995
So, does this event actually&nbsp;
level the playing field?

00:01:03.932 --> 00:01:05.600
There's three components to race.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.223
There's the driving, the&nbsp;equipment, 
and the push.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.223 --> 00:01:14.555
Three main components to bobsledding&nbsp;
and you do not mention braking at all.

00:01:14.555 --> 00:01:18.407
From the outside, all types of&nbsp; bobsledding 
look straight forward.

00:01:18.407 --> 00:01:22.885
Athletes push a sled, jump in, and let&nbsp; gravity 
pull them down an icy, windy track

00:01:22.885 --> 00:01:27.317
at speeds averaging 135 kilometers per hour.

00:01:27.317 --> 00:01:31.440
After 4 runs, the times are added&nbsp; together 
and the fastest sled wins.

00:01:32.697 --> 00:01:34.972
The run can look smooth to spectators

00:01:34.972 --> 00:01:38.520
but inside the sled it’s a&nbsp;
brutal and technical event.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.463
Like being put in a trashcan&nbsp;
and just thrown off a hill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.463 --> 00:01:43.035
Yes, bobsled is terrifying.

00:01:44.246 --> 00:01:48.200
Bobsled athletes traditionally&nbsp;competed 
in teams of two or four.&nbsp;

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.200
The women in pairs. The men in pairs and quartets.

00:01:52.286 --> 00:01:54.349
The person at the front is the pilot.

00:01:54.349 --> 00:01:57.069
They navigate the sled down the&nbsp;
track using a pulley system.

00:01:57.069 --> 00:01:59.292
If you want to go left, you pull towards the left.

00:01:59.292 --> 00:02:01.240
If you want to go right,&nbsp;
you pull towards the right.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.914
But they also have to make sure their&nbsp;
navigating the run as smoothly as possible.

00:02:05.914 --> 00:02:09.040
You want to make sure that you're&nbsp;
not driving so hard that you're

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.080
cutting the ice and causing&nbsp;
all this friction on the ice.&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.297
Sleds move so quickly down the track,&nbsp;
that pilots have to rely on muscle memory,&nbsp;

00:02:16.297 --> 00:02:19.880
You have to make sure you memorize and&nbsp;
know each and every track as a pilot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.092
They're all different, you know.

00:02:21.092 --> 00:02:25.017
Some have 20 curves, some have 15, some&nbsp;
have 14, the curves go different ways.

00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:28.400
In a 4-person sled, the two&nbsp;
center athletes are push-athletes

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.280
named because they help&nbsp;
push the sled at the start.&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.245
And the athlete at the back&nbsp;
is the brakeman but...&nbsp;

00:02:34.245 --> 00:02:36.793
The brakeman does not pull&nbsp;
the brakes on the way down. &nbsp;

00:02:36.793 --> 00:02:40.697
Their job is to help push the sled and get&nbsp;
it up to speed at the beginning of the race.

00:02:40.697 --> 00:02:42.492
Get in and get down.

00:02:42.492 --> 00:02:46.423
Where their head, as low as&nbsp;possible, 
aerodynamic as possible.&nbsp;

00:02:46.423 --> 00:02:47.840
And... maybe pray?

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.743
All the way down, just please don't crash,&nbsp;
please don't crash, please don't crash.&nbsp;

00:02:50.743 --> 00:02:52.800
Oh, and pull the brake at the end.

00:02:54.743 --> 00:02:57.280
Despite the similarities,&nbsp;
the 3 types of bobsledding

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.120
are much different in terms&nbsp;
of equipment and technique.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.532
The four-man sled kind of feels&nbsp;
more like driving a Mack truck.

00:03:03.532 --> 00:03:06.160
The two-man is probably more like a sports car

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:12.110
and monobob is probably in this&nbsp;
comparison more like a motorcycle on ice.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:15.000
All sleds have specific minimum&nbsp;
and maximum weight limits.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.174
The heavier the sled, the faster you go, but&nbsp;
the harder it is to get going at the beginning.

00:03:20.174 --> 00:03:21.977
There are a few other regulations for safety.

00:03:21.977 --> 00:03:24.080
Like how thick and warm the runners can be

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.246
the overall size and material used 
to make the sled.

00:03:27.246 --> 00:03:29.520
But other than that, 2 and 4-person bob teams

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.000
are allowed to adjust their&nbsp;
sleds to maximize aerodynamics.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.046
It's kind of like NASCAR.

00:03:34.046 --> 00:03:37.720
They have rules and regulations&nbsp;
for every bit of the sled.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:42.128
But of course, every mechanic tries to&nbsp;
find the gray area and bend that rule.

00:03:42.128 --> 00:03:45.943
And bending the rules is a lot&nbsp;easier 
when money is on your side.

00:03:45.943 --> 00:03:49.915
Top teams can pay engineers to&nbsp;
research and design faster sleds.

00:03:49.915 --> 00:03:54.550
They tap into technology from Formula 1&nbsp;
racing and even aerospace engineering.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:59.510
When all is said and done, the price tag&nbsp;
for a sled could be over $100,000 USD.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:04.880
Previous years, we were in BMW two-man&nbsp;
sleds and they cost over $250,000 each

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.448
with research and development&nbsp;
and people like that.

00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:11.396
The technological advantage speaks for itself.

00:04:11.396 --> 00:04:15.480
If you look at a list of teams that have medaled&nbsp;
at the Olympics for bobsledding over the years

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.898
it gets repetitive very quickly

00:04:17.898 --> 00:04:20.671
with 5&nbsp;countries really dominating 
the podium.

00:04:20.671 --> 00:04:22.960
So unless you can put that kind of money behind it

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.000
you're going to be defeated before&nbsp;
you even get to the starting blocks.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.841
But not for the monobob.

00:04:30.955 --> 00:04:33.783
The monobob, everybody has the exact same sled.

00:04:33.783 --> 00:04:38.801
A monobob sled costs around $15,000,
a much more affordable price tag.

00:04:38.801 --> 00:04:42.606
Not to mention countries only need to&nbsp;
train and fund travel for a single athlete

00:04:42.606 --> 00:04:45.000
which makes the sport much more accessible.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:46.042
So in one way...

00:04:46.042 --> 00:04:51.830
It levels the playing field and it really just&nbsp;
leaves the sport into pushing and driving.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:54.120
This has already proven to change the game.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.860
In the 2021 World Series athletes from&nbsp;
Jamaica and Brazil both earned medals.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:04:59.501
But...

00:04:59.501 --> 00:05:04.555
The downside of the monobob is it only gives&nbsp;
one more medal opportunity to the pilot.

00:05:04.555 --> 00:05:09.120
Which means women still only get half
the opportunity to medal that men get.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.042
And for us, especially in the US,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.042 --> 00:05:14.800
we have a lot of really great athletes&nbsp;
that come into the sport year after year.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.743
But without that opportunity to medal again at&nbsp;
the Games, you know, we lose a lot of people.

00:05:19.743 --> 00:05:21.520
Plus the amount of work that goes into moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.737
and prepping sleds behind the scenes&nbsp;
requires more than a single athlete.

00:05:25.737 --> 00:05:28.395
It's a really a team effort and&nbsp;
that's what kind of breaks my heart.

00:05:28.395 --> 00:05:29.840
We can't do this without them.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.178
But you're not going to see them on the podium.

00:05:33.177 --> 00:05:35.600
Adding the monobob did give&nbsp;
women another opportunity.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:39.596
But there’s no denying that&nbsp;
it’s not equal, not yet.

00:05:39.596 --> 00:05:43.755
But the sport itself hasn’t&nbsp;
ever been equal. Not really.

00:05:43.755 --> 00:05:47.040
It does open a door for more participation.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:53.556
Do I think it's right? No, but I just don't&nbsp;
think there's enough for the men to even start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.556 --> 00:05:56.000
to even have a fight for the women.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.023
Bobsledding prices are so high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.023 --> 00:06:02.521
that many nations can’t even afford to&nbsp;
send a 4-person men's team to compete.

00:06:02.521 --> 00:06:07.200
I would have loved to see it for the women&nbsp;
and you know them trying to go for four-man.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.440
I think it would have just been really&nbsp;
boring because it would have been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.251
same countries winning every weekend,&nbsp;
the ones that can afford it, right?

00:06:13.251 --> 00:06:16.263
So it’s a step in the right direction, and for now

00:06:16.263 --> 00:06:19.685
at least for the monobob, may the best woman win.

